-- ppwr-packaging-obligations-eu
UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyC$# EU Packaging and Packaging Waste Regulation (PPWR): obligations for packaging placed on the EU market

If you place packaged products on the EU market, the Packaging and Packaging Waste Regulation (PPWR), Regulation (EU) 2025/40, is now the single rulebook for your packaging. It covers all packaging and packaging waste regardless of material or origin, sets requirements for manufacturing, composition and the reusable or recoverable nature of packaging, and includes packaging waste management and prevention measures. This guide explains what the PPWR means in practice for businesses selling physical goods into the EU.

**Short answer:** The PPWR (Regulation (EU) 2025/40) is the EU's single directly applicable rulebook for all packaging placed on the EU market. It sets design-for-recycling requirements with recyclability grades from 2030, substance restrictions including PFAS limits for food-contact packaging, harmonised labelling, extended producer responsibility with per-member-state registration, and declaration-of-conformity duties. It generally applies from 12 August 2026.

## Key facts

- The PPWR is an EU regulation, so it applies directly in every member state without national transposition, replacing the old Packaging and Packaging Waste Directive (94/62/EC).
- It covers all packaging and packaging waste regardless of material or origin, including packaging around imported goods.
- The Regulation entered into force on 11 February 2025 and generally applies from 12 August 2026, with certain requirements phasing in later (for example recyclability performance grades from 2030).
- Its objectives include making all packaging on the EU market recyclable in an economically viable way by 2030, safely increasing recycled plastic content, and reducing virgin material use.
- Extended producer responsibility (EPR) requires registration in the national packaging register of every member state where packaging is placed on the market.
- The "producer" for EPR purposes is generally the company first placing the packaged product on a member state's market; distance sellers can be caught directly.
- Delegated and implementing acts continue to add detail, for example on recyclability grades and labelling formats.

## What the PPWR is

The PPWR is an EU regulation, which means it applies directly in every member state without national transposition. It replaces the old Packaging and Packaging Waste Directive (94/62/EC) and creates one harmonised framework across the EU. The Regulation entered into force on 11 February 2025 and generally applies from 12 August 2026, with certain requirements phasing in later. Its objectives include making all packaging on the EU market recyclable in an economically viable way by 2030, safely increasing recycled plastic content, and reducing virgin material use.

The shift from directive to regulation is the structural point. Under the old directive, each member state transposed packaging rules into national law, producing 27 variants with different registers, fee structures and enforcement practices. Under the PPWR, the core requirements are identical everywhere because a regulation applies directly. National differences do not disappear entirely (EPR registers and fee collection remain nationally administered), but the substantive duties, design requirements, labelling and phase-in dates are now single-text.

The regulation's scope is deliberately broad: all packaging and packaging waste regardless of material (plastic, paper and board, glass, metal, wood, composites) and regardless of origin. Packaging is understood in the regulation's categories of sales packaging, grouped packaging and transport packaging, and the obligations can differ by category. Read the definitions first, because scoping errors (treating transport packaging as out of scope, for example) are the most common early mistakes.

## Who it applies to

The PPWR applies to economic operators across the packaging chain: packaging manufacturers, importers who bring packaged goods into the EU, distributors, and the producers who place packaged products on the market. If you are a non-EU brand selling packaged goods to EU customers, for example through your own webshop or a marketplace, the obligations attach to your packaging too, typically through your EU importer or fulfilment arrangements.

The pivotal concept is the "producer" for EPR purposes: generally the company first placing the packaged product on a member state's market. For a brand selling through EU distribution, the producer may be the EU importer or the brand's EU entity depending on the chain. For distance sellers shipping directly to consumers in a member state, the seller itself can be the producer in that state, which means EPR registration there even with no local establishment. Marketplaces do not automatically absorb this; the underlying seller's producer status must be determined per member state of sale.

Packaging manufacturers have their own direct duties (design requirements, substance restrictions, declaration of conformity, technical documentation), distinct from the producer's EPR duties. A company that both manufactures packaging and places packaged products on the market carries both sets. Map your position in the chain per member state, because the same company can be a distributor in one market and the producer in another.

## Key obligations

1. **Design for recycling.** Packaging must be designed for material recycling and, when it becomes waste, be capable of being collected, sorted and recycled at scale. A system of recyclability performance grades applies from 2030, with EPR fees modulated by grade, which turns recyclability into a direct cost variable. Design decisions such as material choice, multi-material combinations and separable components should be assessed against the design-for-recycling criteria the regulation and its delegated acts set.

2. **Substance restrictions.** Packaging must minimise harmful substances, with the regulation restricting substances of concern in packaging. Restrictions on PFAS in food-contact packaging are part of the framework. As with product chemicals, this is a supplier-data exercise: composition declarations from packaging suppliers, backed by testing for high-risk applications such as food contact.

3. **Weight and volume minimisation.** Packaging must be reduced to the minimum necessary weight and volume for its function, and the regulation addresses excessive packaging, including empty-space limits for certain packaging categories. E-commerce sellers should review box-size assortments and void-fill practices against these provisions, because the oversized-box habit is exactly what the rules target.

4. **Labelling.** Harmonised labelling requirements help consumers sort packaging correctly. The regulation moves toward EU-wide harmonised labels indicating material composition and sorting instructions, replacing the patchwork of national sorting marks. Artwork planning should track the implementing acts that define the label formats, and labels should be version-controlled against the phase-in dates.

5. **Extended producer responsibility (EPR).** Producers must register in the national packaging register of every member state where they place packaging on the market and finance collection and recycling. Registration is per member state, not per EU, and fee structures differ nationally. Distance sellers placing packaged products directly with consumers in a member state are typically producers there.

6. **Waste prevention and reuse.** The Regulation sets waste prevention measures and reuse provisions, including reusability expectations for transport and sales packaging. For certain flows, such as transport packaging between businesses, reuse targets and systems change the economics of single-use packaging; assess where your packaging flows fall under reuse provisions.

7. **Declaration of conformity.** Where the Regulation requires it, manufacturers draw up an EU declaration of conformity for the packaging, supported by technical documentation. This formalises packaging as a regulated product: the declaration, the technical file and the underlying evidence (design-for-recycling assessment, substance data) must exist and be producible.

8. **Separate collection and deposit return.** The regulation sets separate-collection objectives for beverage packaging, with deposit return schemes (DRS) as the default mechanism for single-use plastic beverage bottles and metal beverage containers, targeting high collection rates by 2029. Beverage brands need a DRS strategy per member state; this is one of the most operationally demanding parts of the regulation for the drinks sector.

## EPR in practice: registration per member state

EPR is where the PPWR meets national administration. The substantive duty (register, report, pay) is harmonised, but the registers, portals, reporting formats, fee schedules and deadlines are national. A seller active in ten member states holds ten registrations, files ten sets of reports and pays ten fee schedules. There is no EU-wide one-stop shop.

The practical program: first, determine producer status per member state of sale (first placer, including distance-selling flows). Second, register in each national packaging register before placing packaging on that market; keep the registration confirmations in a per-country file. Third, report packaging quantities by material per the national format and schedule; this requires SKU-level packaging data (component, material, weight) across all parcel configurations. Fourth, pay the EPR fees, which are increasingly modulated by recyclability grade, so design choices flow directly into the fee line. Fifth, calendar the national deadlines separately; they do not align.

Service providers can consolidate the administration, but the legal duty remains with the producer. Verify that registrations and reports are in your company's name and that you receive the underlying evidence. Under-declared quantities are a standard enforcement target because registers cross-check volumes.

## The phase-in timeline

| Milestone | What happens |
|---|---|
| 11 February 2025 | Regulation enters into force |
| 12 August 2026 | General application date: core obligations apply |
| 2028-2029 | Further provisions phase in, including separate-collection and DRS-related duties for beverage packaging |
| 2030 | Recyclability performance grades apply; key recyclability and recycled-content objectives target this horizon |

The table is a planning aid, not the law. Individual articles carry their own application dates, and delegated and implementing acts add operative detail (for example the exact recyclability grade criteria and label formats). Maintain an article-level applicability calendar for your packaging categories, and diary the delegated-act publications that affect your materials.

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Inventory every packaging component you place on the EU market (primary, secondary, transport), per SKU and parcel configuration.
- [ ] Determine producer status per member state of sale, including distance-selling flows.
- [ ] Confirm each component is designed for recycling and can be collected, sorted and recycled at scale; assess against the design-for-recycling criteria.
- [ ] Check substance compliance, including PFAS restrictions for food-contact packaging; collect supplier composition declarations.
- [ ] Review packaging weight, volume and empty-space ratios against the minimisation provisions.
- [ ] Register for packaging EPR in each member state of sale and arrange fee payments; keep per-country files.
- [ ] Set up quantity reporting by material per national format and schedule.
- [ ] Prepare the EU declaration of conformity and technical documentation for your packaging where required.
- [ ] Apply the required harmonised labels to your packaging; version-control artwork against phase-in dates.
- [ ] Track phase-in dates for later requirements, such as the 2030 recyclability grades and DRS duties for beverage packaging.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, the PPWR turns packaging from a procurement detail into a regulated product in its own right. Budget for EPR fees in every EU country you sell into, because registration is per member state, not per EU. Start supplier conversations early: you need composition data and recyclability evidence from packaging suppliers to complete your declaration of conformity. If you sell the same product in the EU and elsewhere, consider standardising on PPWR-compliant packaging globally rather than running two packaging lines. However, note that member-state enforcement practices and EPR fee structures still differ, so local advice remains valuable. The strategic opportunity is that recyclability-graded fees reward better design directly: packaging engineered for recycling pays lower EPR fees, which makes the design investment measurable in the P&L.

## How the EU compares with other markets

The **EU** now has a single directly applicable packaging regulation. The **United Kingdom** operates its own separate packaging EPR scheme, so UK sales need a parallel registration, not a PPWR one. In the **United States** there is no federal packaging EPR law; several states have enacted their own packaging laws with different scopes and timelines. A product compliant in the EU is not automatically compliant in the UK or the US, and vice versa. For multi-market sellers, this means three separate packaging compliance tracks (EU/PPWR, UK EPR, US state laws), with the EU track being the most comprehensively codified.

**Frequently asked questions:**

## Does the PPWR apply to packaging around imported goods?

Yes, it covers all packaging placed on the EU market regardless of origin. Imported goods' packaging is in scope, with obligations typically attaching through the EU importer or the distance seller.

## Who is the "producer" for EPR purposes?

Generally the company first placing the packaged product on a member state's market; distance sellers can be caught directly. Map producer status per member state, because the same company can hold different roles in different markets.

## Do I need a separate EPR registration per EU country?

Yes, registration is national. There is no EU-wide one-stop shop; each member state has its own register, reporting format, fee schedule and deadlines.

## What happens to the old Packaging Directive?

The PPWR replaces Directive 94/62/EC. National transpositions give way to the directly applicable regulation as its provisions take effect.

## What are the recyclability performance grades?

A grading system for packaging recyclability that applies from 2030, defined in detail through delegated acts. EPR fees are modulated by grade, so higher-recyclability packaging costs less in fees. Assess current designs against the design-for-recycling criteria now.

## Do beverage bottles need deposit return schemes?

The regulation sets separate-collection objectives for single-use plastic beverage bottles and metal beverage containers, with DRS as the default mechanism targeting high collection rates by 2029. Beverage brands should build a per-member-state DRS strategy.

## Assumptions and limitations

- This guide summarises the framework as of the last-verified date; delegated and implementing acts continue to add detail (for example on recyclability grades and labelling formats).
- Fee levels, registration portals and enforcement practice are set nationally and are outside the scope of this guide.
- This guide does not cover deposit return scheme operational detail or sector-specific packaging rules in individual member states beyond the framework described.

## Official sources

- [Regulation (EU) 2025/40 on EUR-Lex (English)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2025/40/oj/eng)
- [Regulation (EU) 2025/40, PDF edition](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2025/40/oj/eng/pdf)
- [European Commission: Packaging waste overview](https://environment.ec.europa.eu/topics/waste-and-recycling/packaging-waste_en?prefLang=ro)

Last verified: 2026-09-29.

